# Proposed mailmap for the extraction. NOT applied yet - decide first.
#
# Every one of the 279 commits in tools/paulikit's history carries
# mkhellat@gmail.com. Meanwhile CITATION.cff and pyproject.toml credit
# four authors at @beavernets.com. A reviewer who compares the two
# sees a single-author Gmail project claiming four institutional
# authors, and asks why.
#
# Nothing here is dishonest either way - you wrote the code, and the
# co-authors are credited for the work as a whole. The question is
# only which identity the published history should carry.
#
# THREE OPTIONS:
#
# 1. Do nothing. History keeps mkhellat@gmail.com. Honest, and the
#    mismatch is explainable in one sentence if anyone asks. Costs
#    nothing and rewrites nothing.
#
# 2. Apply this mailmap during extraction:
#       git filter-repo --subdirectory-filter tools/paulikit \
#                       --mailmap .mailmap-proposed
#    Rewrites the author address on all 279 commits to the
#    institutional one. Do this only if @beavernets.com is genuinely
#    your identity for this work - retroactively reassigning authorship
#    to an address you did not use is the kind of thing that looks
#    worse than the problem it solves.
#
# 3. Ship this file as a plain .mailmap in the new repository without
#    rewriting anything. Git and GitHub then DISPLAY the canonical
#    address while the underlying commits keep their original one.
#    This is what .mailmap is actually designed for, it is not a
#    history rewrite, and it is reversible. Recommended.
#
# Option 3 is the default recommendation: it fixes the display
# mismatch, leaves the record intact, and is the conventional use of
# the mechanism.

